        - [APPLIED] Loop D independence assumption
            - rng.uniform(0, 1) under H0 gives independent uniform p-values. The 64% naive FWER is correct under independence, but real guardrail metrics are correlated (sessions, clicks, time-on-page all share variance).
            - Add a single sentence noting that 64% is the independence upper bound; correlated guardrails inflate less, but the qualitative story is unchanged.
            - Resolution: added independence-upper-bound note + correlated-guardrails caveat in Loop D Observe step.
        - [APPLIED] Loop D Bayesian paragraph (highest priority)
            - Current text: "a posterior over the joint of all 20 metric lifts. Reading marginals does not blow up the family-wise rate -- the prior + likelihood machinery accounts for the joint already."
            - This is misleading. A flat-prior Bayesian marginal credible interval excluding zero behaves much like the naive frequentist test; there is no built-in family-wise control.
            - The actual mechanism is a hierarchical prior that shrinks all 20 lifts toward zero (partial pooling). Shrinkage is what damps the false-alarm rate, not "Bayesian machinery" generically.
            - Rewrite to (a) name hierarchical / partial-pooling priors explicitly, (b) say flat-prior Bayes does not protect, (c) frame the choice as decision-theoretic (loss over the joint) rather than a free lunch.
            - This aligns with the project rule that PyMC is the canonical Bayesian path: the rewrite should sketch the hierarchical model, not hand-wave.
            - Resolution: replaced the misleading paragraph with a three-part rewrite. (a) flat-prior Bayes does not protect and reproduces the family-wise blow-up. (b) hierarchical Bayes with partial pooling shrinks individual lifts toward the population mean, and that shrinkage is what damps false alarms. (c) included a PyMC-style sketch (mu_lift, tau_lift hyperprior, lift_i ~ Normal(mu_lift, tau_lift)) and framed the decision as a loss over the joint posterior, not a marginal-CI count.
